VERDICT: DON'T SHIP

1. Permission refusal is correct: #17 outweighs stop intent. Escape would answer “No,” so `/interrupt` must press nothing. Lock-layer acceptance requires accepting the request, not sending a key.
2. The message is misleading for permissions: “nothing was pressed” is accurate, but it should say “Remote stop is blocked because dismissing this permission would answer it; resolve it on the Mac.”
3. Numbered-list detection can falsely classify generated transcript text as CHOICE. “Below composer close” alone is insufficient and causes a real generation to become remotely uninterruptible.
4. Reuse the fingerprinted `choice` classifier: require known menu chrome, selectable-row/highlight markers, bottom anchoring, and exact supported option structure—not merely numbered text.
5. Unknown menu fallthrough is unsafe: a new permission layout would receive Escape, violating #17. Refuse when there is structural evidence of an interactive modal but no recognized fingerprint; do not refuse every unknown non-composer screen.
6. Overlay detection must run before CHOICE detection. A `/tasks` overlay may contain numbered rows; current ordering can return `choice-open` instead of closing the overlay as specified.
